===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|cheker}}, aphetic from {{der|en|xno|escheker||chessboard}}, from {{der|en|la|scaccarium}}, thus ultimately morpheme-for-morpheme cognate to etymology 1.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A playing piece in the game of [[checkers]] (British: [[draughts]]), or certain other table games such as [[backgammon]].
# A pattern of alternating colours as on a chessboard.
# An individual square appearing in such a pattern.

=====Derived terms=====
{{col|en|green-white-checker|bechecker|checkerist|checkerspot|checkerwise|checkerwork|checkerboard}}

=====Related terms=====
* {{l|en|exchequer}}

=====Translations=====
{{trans-see|draught}}

====Verb====
[[File:Checkered flags-fr.svg|thumb|A flag with a '''checker''' pattern]]
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[mark]] in a pattern of [[alternating]] light and dark positions, like a [[checkerboard]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[develop]] markings in a pattern of alternating light and dark positions, like a checkerboard.

===Etymology 3===
Alteration of {{m|en|chequer}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# The fruit of the [[wild service tree]] or [[chequer tree]], {{taxlink|Photinia villosa|species}}, syn. {{taxlink|Sorbus terminalis|species}}

=====Usage notes=====
* Almost always spelled [[chequer]], because the tree itself is native to areas where that spelling is used, and is pretty much unknown in areas that use the other spelling.

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|checkerberry}}
*{{l|en|checkerbloom}}
*{{l|en|checkermallow}}

